\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{No reflection, and a negative weight}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{G} be the graph on vertices
#{\{0,1,2,3,4,5\}} with edges
#{\{01,02,03,12,14,35\}}. Its bidirected graph, with every
weight equal to #{1}, has trivial automorphism group. The
identity fixes every edge, so the twisted form of
\ref{fdip-00Q1} equals the derived form and reverses no
direction.}

\p{Separately, let two vertices be joined by one edge of
weight #{w=-a} with #{a>0}, and let the precision be
#{Q=\extd^\dagger W\extd+mI} with #{m>2a}. Then #{Q} is
positive definite, but the swap of the two vertices is not
reflection positive.}

\proof{
\p{The degrees in #{G} are #{3,3,2,2,1,1}. The leaf at #{4}
meets a vertex of degree #{3}, and the leaf at #{5} meets a
vertex of degree #{2}, so both leaves are fixed by every
automorphism. The degree #{3} vertex adjacent to #{4} is then
fixed, and so is the other degree #{3} vertex. The remaining
two vertices are distinguished by adjacency to #{5}. Thus
only the identity remains. A bidirected automorphism induces
an automorphism of #{G}, so the bidirected graph is equally
rigid.}

\p{In the two-vertex example the precision matrix is
##{Q=\begin{pmatrix}m-a&a\\a&m-a\end{pmatrix}.}
Its determinant is #{m(m-2a)>0}, and both diagonal entries
are positive, so #{Q} is positive definite. The covariance is
a positive multiple of
##{\begin{pmatrix}m-a&-a\\-a&m-a\end{pmatrix},}
whose off-diagonal entry is negative. For the swap, the
positive half may be taken to be one vertex. The coordinate
function at that vertex pairs with its reflected copy by the
off-diagonal covariance, which is negative.}
}
